            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "A scaled elu: for positive values, 1.0507 times the input; for negative values, 1.0507 times 1.6733 times (e to the x, minus 1).\n\nThe two constants are chosen so that a signal passed through many layers of a network keeps roughly the same average and spread instead of growing or dying away - \"self-normalising\". They are fixed; there is nothing to adjust. Outside a network it is simply elu, slightly stretched.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAbove zero a straight line slightly STEEPER than the input (4 gives 4.20), so it pulls away from the diagonal on the right. Below zero an exponential that levels off at about -1.76 (-4 gives -1.73). There is a corner at 0: the slope is 1.05 on the right and 1.76 on the left.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.selu\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -4 (left edge of the plot) to 4 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -4 to 4; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.elu is the same shape with an adjustable floor and no scaling. t.nn.celu is elu without the corner. t.nn.relu is flat at 0 below zero.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 780,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 705,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "A scaled elu: for positive values, 1.0507 times the input; for negative values, 1.0507 times 1.6733 times (e to the x, minus 1).\n\nThe two constants are chosen so that a signal passed through many layers of a network keeps roughly the same average and spread instead of growing or dying away - \"self-normalising\". They are fixed; there is nothing to adjust. Outside a network it is simply elu, slightly stretched.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAbove zero a straight line slightly STEEPER than the input (4 gives 4.20), so it pulls away from the diagonal on the right. Below zero an exponential that levels off at about -1.76 (-4 gives -1.73). There is a corner at 0: the slope is 1.05 on the right and 1.76 on the left.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.selu\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -4 (left edge of the plot) to 4 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -4 to 4; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.elu is the same shape with an adjustable floor and no scaling. t.nn.celu is elu without the corner. t.nn.relu is flat at 0 below zero."
        },
